Make an almost-unchanged VP8 frame cheap: copy only what changed.

Applied after libvpx-vp8-active-map-early-out.patch, which it builds on.

That patch stopped the encoder *thinking* about macroblocks the active map
excludes. It did not stop it *copying* them. Sampled on a real O2 (R10000,
195 MHz) with tools/pcsample.c, a 1280x1024 frame in which a pointer moved --
seven or eight active macroblocks out of 5120 -- took a median 190 ms, and
three quarters of that was moving pixels that had not changed:

   init_encode_frame_mb_context   ~65 ms   the early-out patch's own part (2):
                                           the whole reference copied into
                                           the reconstruction buffer, every
                                           frame
   vp8_copy_and_extend_frame      ~65 ms   the lookahead taking a full copy of
                                           the source, every frame
   vp8_copy_mem16x16 (encode_mb_row) ~23 ms  each source macroblock copied to
                                           a scratch buffer, all 5120 of them

That floor is the pointer: every frame pays it however little changed, so it
is both the pointer's frame rate and its latency.

Three changes:

1. The lookahead copies only the active part of the source -- which libvpx
   already has code for, and never runs. The partial copy is written for a
   queue of one buffer, and vp8_lookahead_init adds one to every depth "to keep
   the last frame", so lag_in_frames 0 makes a queue of two and `max_sz == 1`
   never holds. With two buffers written in turn, the one being written last
   held the frame from two pushes ago, and what has changed since is exactly
   what this frame's map and the previous frame's map mark. Copying their union
   brings the WHOLE buffer up to date, not just its active part -- which
   matters, because a few things read the entire source (the skin map, for
   one). Anything that breaks the chain -- no map, a flagged frame, the first
   two pushes -- takes the full copy as before and restarts it.

2. The reconstruction buffer is seeded from LAST only where the two differ.
   The buffer being written is a free one holding an earlier frame on LAST's
   chain (in steady state, the one before LAST), so it already matches LAST
   except in the macroblocks coded since. The encoder now keeps, per frame
   buffer, which frame it holds (fb_stamp, set only when a frame becomes LAST,
   so a dropped frame's buffer is never trusted) and, per macroblock, the last
   frame that coded it (mb_stamp; all_stamp for a key frame, a frame without a
   map, or the loop filter, which touches every macroblock). The seed copies
   what is newer than the buffer and nothing else, and falls back to copying
   the whole visible area whenever the record cannot be trusted.

3. encode_mb_row (and its threaded twin) skips the scratch copy of an excluded
   macroblock, which vp8cx_encode_inter_macroblock returns before reading.

Measured on the O2 with ports/rust/agent-portable/src/bin/encfloor.rs, a
24x24 square moving with the map covering its old and new position (median of
60 frames, two runs each):

                  before      after
   1280x1024      190 ms      46 ms     (4.1x)
    640x512        55 ms      16 ms     (3.4x)

THE BITSTREAM IS UNCHANGED. `encfloor verify` runs a deliberately awkward
sequence -- the pointer, windows, full-width bands, frames with an empty map,
frames with no map, forced key frames -- and hashes every encoded byte. Built
against libvpx with and without this patch, on the O2:

   640x512, 600 frames     1579270 bytes, hash fb4c9ed6 both    51.3 s -> 29.5 s
   1280x1024, 300 frames   2355057 bytes, hash 2966bffe both    87.9 s -> 51.6 s

A wrong seed or a stale source anywhere a later macroblock predicted from would
have changed the bytes of every frame after it.

